WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.500
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Are you migrating

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.840
your monolithic application
to microservices on GKE?

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.170
Then I bet that data is a
huge part of that migration.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.420
Stay tuned to find out
more in today's episode

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.979
of Get Cooking in Cloud.

00:00:11.979 --> 00:00:15.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.347 --> 00:00:22.430
CARTER MORGAN: When it
comes time to migrate data,

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:24.740
the strategy you use
depends on the answers

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:25.948
to a few important questions.

00:00:25.948 --> 00:00:27.323
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well, how much

00:00:27.323 --> 00:00:28.910
data do you need to migrate?

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:30.890
CARTER MORGAN: How often
does this data change?

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:32.807
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Can
you afford the downtime

00:00:32.807 --> 00:00:34.610
represented by the
cut-over window

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:36.230
while migrating that data?

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:39.120
CARTER MORGAN: What is your
current data consistency model?

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.720
There's really no single best
approach to migrating data.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.000
Today, we will share four
different data migration

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.718
approaches, each of which
tackles different issues

00:00:46.718 --> 00:00:49.010
depending on the scale and
the requirements of the data

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:49.790
migration.

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:51.707
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: The
data migration approach

00:00:51.707 --> 00:00:53.600
you use is based on the
cut-over window size

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.430
that your application
can tolerate,

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:58.310
the refactoring effort
that your team can handle,

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:01.002
and the amount of flexibility
each approach offers.

00:01:01.002 --> 00:01:02.960
CARTER MORGAN: The
scheduled maintenance period

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.870
is ideal if your workloads
can afford a cut-over window.

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:08.030
It's scheduled in the sense
that you can plan when

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:09.800
the cut-over window occurs.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.450
Here's how it looks.

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:13.940
Copy data from legacy
sites to new site

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:15.770
in order to minimize
the cut-over window.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:17.810
For more details, refer
to the earlier episode

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:19.850
on migrating in stages.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:21.780
Compare the legacy data
against the new data

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:24.680
set to make sure there
weren't any copy errors.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.750
Stop further data from
being read into the system

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:28.220
during the migration.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:31.310
Synchronize any changes that
happen after the initial copy.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:33.650
Deploy new versions of
the workloads and services

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:35.390
that use a new site.

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:37.248
Start the workloads
and services.

00:01:37.248 --> 00:01:39.290
Retire the legacy site
when you no longer need it

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:41.470
as a fallback option.

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:44.170
This approach places most of the
burden on the operations side,

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:46.660
because minimal refactoring
of the workload and system

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:47.160
is needed.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:48.660
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Not all workloads

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:50.470
can afford a long
cut-over window,

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:52.420
so the continuous
replication approach

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:54.490
builds on the scheduled
maintenance approach

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:56.320
by providing a
continuous replication

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:59.960
mechanism after the initial
copy and validation steps.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.570
Here's what that
would look like.

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:04.420
Copy data from legacy
site to the new site

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:06.520
in order to minimize
the cut-over window,

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:09.070
just like we did in the
scheduled maintenance approach.

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:12.190
Then, we compare the legacy
data against the new data

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:14.980
to make sure there
weren't any copy errors.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:18.490
Set up a continuous replication
mechanism from the legacy site

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:19.810
to the new site.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:22.630
Stop further data from
being written to the system

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:24.250
during the migration.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:27.850
Refactor workloads and
services to use the new site

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:31.540
and wait for the replication to
fully synchronize the new site

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:33.040
with the legacy site.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:35.650
Then, start the
workloads and services.

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:38.260
Retire the legacy site
when you no longer need it

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:40.390
as a fallback option.

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:43.240
This approach is more complex
than the scheduled maintenance

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:45.400
approach, but it
minimizes the time

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:48.190
for the required cut-over
window by minimizing

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:50.748
the amount of data you
need to synchronize.

00:02:50.748 --> 00:02:52.540
CARTER MORGAN: If your
workloads have hard,

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:55.123
high availability requirements
and you can't afford a cut-over

00:02:55.123 --> 00:02:57.790
window, a different approach--
what we're calling the Y

00:02:57.790 --> 00:02:58.600
recipe--

00:02:58.600 --> 00:02:59.997
would make sense.

00:02:59.997 --> 00:03:02.080
In this recipe, the workload
is writing or reading

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:04.330
data in both the legacy
site and the new site

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:05.950
during the migration.

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:07.390
Here's how it looks.

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:09.280
Refactor subsystems
to write data

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:11.320
both to the legacy
and the new site

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.750
while reading from
the legacy site.

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:15.340
Copy data into the
new system that

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:18.440
was written before the new
site began to write data.

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:20.390
Compare the legacy data
against the new data

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:22.970
set to make sure there
weren't any errors.

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:26.690
Switch read operations from the
legacy site to the new site.

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:29.270
Perform another round of data
validation and consistency

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:30.680
to check data.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:33.392
Disable writing in
the legacy site,

00:03:33.392 --> 00:03:35.600
and retire the legacy site
when you no longer need it

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:37.700
as a fallback option.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:40.370
Unlike the scheduled maintenance
and continuous replication

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:43.090
approaches, the Y writing
and reading recipe

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:45.320
shifts most of the effort
from the operations side

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:48.230
to the development side due
to the multiple refactorings,

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:50.670
but it does not require
a cut-over window,

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:51.800
which is a plus.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:53.258
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Now, if you want

00:03:53.258 --> 00:03:55.550
to reduce the large amount
of refactoring effort needed

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:57.620
in the Y approach,
you can centralize

00:03:57.620 --> 00:03:59.690
the data read and
write operations

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:03.620
by refactoring the workloads
and services to use data access

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:04.910
microservice.

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:07.820
This scalable microservice
becomes the only entry point

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:09.680
to your data storage
there, and it

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:11.780
acts as a proxy for that layer.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:14.000
It's actually pretty
similar to the Y approach

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:15.320
we discussed earlier.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:17.810
The only difference is
instead of refactoring

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:20.990
the entire service, we
focus the refactoring effort

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:23.520
on the data access
microservice alone.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:25.850
Refactor the data
access microservice

00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:29.840
to write data both in the
legacy site and the new site.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:32.680
Reads are performed
against the legacy site.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:34.520
Identify the data
that was written

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:37.250
before you enabled the
writes in the new site

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:40.370
and copy it from the legacy
site to the new site.

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:42.320
Along with the
refactoring above,

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:45.700
this ensures that the
data stores are aligned.

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:48.740
Perform data validation
and consistency checks,

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:50.690
comparing data in
the legacy site

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:52.700
against data in the new site.

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:55.070
Refactor the data
access microservice

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:57.020
to read from the new site.

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:59.990
Perform another round of data
validation and consistency

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:02.810
checks comparing data
in the legacy site

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:04.820
against data in the new site.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:08.060
Refactor the data access
microservice to write only

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:09.540
in the legacy site.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.660
When you no longer
need the legacy site

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:14.810
as the fallback option
anymore, retire it.

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:17.300
In a microservice
architecture, this recipe

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:19.130
is the preferred
method and gives you

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:22.610
the maximum flexibility, because
you can refactor this data

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:24.740
access component
without impacting

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:26.660
other components
of the architecture

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:29.450
and without requiring
a cut-over window.

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:31.940
It does put a burden
on the developer side,

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:35.520
but it's significantly less than
the Y approach we discussed.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.480
CARTER MORGAN: So in summary,
we looked at four recipes

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:41.390
to migrate data, although
picking the right option

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:42.770
would depend on
weighing the pros

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:44.780
and cons for your situation.

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:47.960
For more information about
migrating a monolith to GKE

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:49.820
and these data
migration strategies,

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:52.460
refer to the article linked in
the description below written

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:54.460
by our friends, [? Marco ?]
[? and ?] [? Teo. ?]

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:56.252
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well, there you have it.

00:05:56.252 --> 00:05:58.700
If you are looking to migrate
a monolithic application

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:00.800
